{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>And on the day of your rejoicing and your appointed seasons, etc.</b> From these verses the Rabbis of blessed memory learned that on Rosh HaShanah one recites Malkhiyot, Zikhronot, and Shofarot, as is found in the Yalkut on this parashah (10, 625). And one must understand what they saw to interpret these verses as referring to Rosh HaShanah. And it appears that they learned this from what is said: ״וְכִי תָבֹאוּ מִלְחָמָה בְּאַרְצְכֶם עַל הַצַּר הַצֹּרֵר״ — 'and when you go to war in your land against the adversary who oppresses' — and this was the practice of old among Israel, that at every time of distress they would decree a fast and they would blow the shofar and sound the teru'ah and recite Malkhiyot, Zikhronot, and Shofarot, as is found in Tur Orach Chayim, siman 579. And perhaps their proof is from these verses that say ״וְכִי תָבֹאוּ מִלְחָמָה״ — 'and when you go to war' — etc. And infer from it a fortiori: on every day that has tekiah, one does according to this order, and Rosh HaShanah is included. And from the fact that it says ״מִלְחָמָה בְּאַרְצְכֶם״ — 'war in your land' — with a bet, infer from it that it speaks of an adversary who is within the land, and this is fitting for Rosh HaShanah, on which one blows the shofar to confound the Satan, the adversary who is within — the inner one who is inside the land — as opposed to an ordinary adversary, who is outside the land. And furthermore: what difference is there between an external adversary and an internal one? [That is to say, the reference to the internal adversary itself signals the deeper meaning.] And according to this, the order of Rosh HaShanah is well alluded to in these verses."], :book :numbers, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {0 "The phrase 'ודן מנה ומנה' (inferred from this and from that) is read here as introducing a fortiori reasoning applied both to the war-verses and to the daily tekiah; the parenthetical gloss reflects the compressed logic. The clause 'ומה לי צורר חיצוני מה לי פנימי' is a rhetorical formula meaning it makes no difference, here left implicit as a pointed question that reinforces the Rosh HaShanah reading."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:40:23.791807730Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}